---
import GuideArticle from '../../../components/GuideArticle.astro';
import { getGuide } from '../../../guides.js';

const guide = getGuide('how-pretax-401k-changes-take-home-pay');
---
<GuideArticle
  title={guide.title}
  description={guide.description}
  pathname={guide.href}
  reviewed="2026-10-10"
  toolHref={guide.toolHref}
  toolLabel={guide.toolLabel}
>
  <p>
    A traditional 401(k) deferral lowers the wages that flow into federal ordinary taxable income. It usually does <em>not</em> lower the wages that Social Security and Medicare see. That split is why take-home pay does not fall one-for-one with the contribution, and why FICA lines on a paycheck can look stubborn when the federal income-tax line moves.
  </p>
  <p>
    Case study: $72,000 salary, single, biweekly, no state tax, tax year 2026. Compare 0% deferral with 10% deferral. Reproduce it on the <a href="/paycheck-calculator/" class="kdp-link">paycheck calculator</a> by changing only the 401(k) percentage.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-normal text-stone-950 pt-4">With 0% deferral</h2>
  <p>
    Taxable income for federal ordinary tax is 72,000 − 16,100 = $55,900. Federal tax is <strong>$7,010</strong>. Social Security is $4,464. Medicare is $1,044. Net pay is <strong>$59,482</strong> a year, about <strong>$2,287.77</strong> every other week.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-normal text-stone-950 pt-4">With 10% deferral</h2>
  <p>
    Ten percent of $72,000 is <strong>$7,200</strong> deferred into the plan for the year ($276.92 per biweekly check). Federal taxable income becomes 72,000 − 7,200 − 16,100 = <strong>$48,700</strong>. That amount still reaches the 12% bracket but no longer touches the 22% bracket:
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>10% × $12,400 = $1,240</li>
    <li>12% × $36,300 = $4,356</li>
    <li>Federal income tax = <strong>$5,596</strong></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Federal tax fell by $1,414 (from $7,010 to $5,596). Social Security and Medicare did not move: still $4,464 and $1,044 on $72,000 of FICA wages under this model. Net pay becomes:
  </p>
  <p class="rounded-xl border border-[#e6d9c8] bg-[#fffdf8] px-4 py-3 font-mono text-[0.95rem] leading-7">
    72,000 − 7,200 − 5,596 − 4,464 − 1,044 = $53,696
  </p>
  <p>
    About <strong>$2,065.23</strong> per biweekly check. You directed $7,200 into retirement accounts and your take-home fell by $59,482 − $53,696 = <strong>$5,786</strong>, not by the full $7,200. The difference is the federal tax you no longer owe this year under this sketch.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-[#e6d9c8] bg-[#fffdf8]">
    <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
      <caption class="sr-only">0% versus 10% traditional 401(k) on $72,000</caption>
      <thead class="bg-[#f3eadc] text-stone-800">
        <tr>
          <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Line</th>
          <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">0% deferral</th>
          <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">10% deferral</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-stone-800">
        <tr class="border-t border-[#e6d9c8]"><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Gross wages</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$72,000</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$72,000</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-t border-[#e6d9c8]"><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Traditional 401(k)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$0</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$7,200</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-t border-[#e6d9c8]"><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Federal taxable income</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$55,900</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$48,700</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-t border-[#e6d9c8]"><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Federal income tax</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$7,010</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$5,596</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-t border-[#e6d9c8]"><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Social Security</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$4,464</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$4,464</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-t border-[#e6d9c8]"><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Medicare</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$1,044</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$1,044</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-t border-[#e6d9c8] font-semibold"><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Net pay</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$59,482</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$53,696</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-t border-[#e6d9c8]"><td class="px-4 py-2.5">Net per biweekly check</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$2,287.77</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">$2,065.23</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-normal text-stone-950 pt-4">What this does not cover</h2>
  <p>
    Roth 401(k) contributions are after-tax for federal ordinary income: they do not shrink this year’s taxable wages the way traditional deferrals do. Employer matches are not employee take-home. Early-withdrawal penalties, required distributions, and investment returns are outside a paycheck model. State tax treatment of deferrals varies; this example uses a 0% state rate.
  </p>


  <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-normal text-stone-950 pt-4">Where the $1,414 of tax savings sits</h2>
  <p>
    Without the deferral, the last $5,500 of taxable income was in the 22% bracket and cost $1,210. After a $7,200 deferral, taxable income falls by $7,200, which removes that entire 22% slice and also pulls $1,700 out of the 12% band ($1,700 × 0.12 = $204). The federal tax drop is 1,210 + 204 = <strong>$1,414</strong>. The first dollars you defer out of a 22% band save 22 cents on the dollar in ordinary tax; once you have dropped fully into the 12% band, further traditional deferrals save 12 cents on the dollar in this sketch (before credits and other moving parts).
  </p>
  <p>
    That is also why “I am in the 22% bracket, so my 401(k) saves 22%” is only true for the dollars that actually leave the 22% band. After those dollars are gone, the marginal benefit steps down with the brackets.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-normal text-stone-950 pt-4">Cash-flow view on one biweekly check</h2>
  <p>
    Gross per check is about $2,769.23. At 10% deferral, $276.92 goes to the plan before federal ordinary tax is computed. Federal withholding in the planning model falls from about $269.62 to about $215.23. FICA stays about $211.85. Net falls from about $2,287.77 to about $2,065.23. The check is lighter by about $222.54, while $276.92 reached the retirement account — the gap is the federal tax not withheld under the annual model.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-normal text-stone-950 pt-4">Assumptions and limitations</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-3 pl-6">
    <li>Traditional pretax 401(k) only, as a flat percent of annual gross.</li>
    <li>FICA wages equal gross wages in this model. Some cafeteria-plan items can differ in real payroll.</li>
    <li>No other pretax benefits (HSA, FSA) in the 10% case.</li>
    <li>No saver’s credit or other credits.</li>
    <li>Plan limits, catch-up contributions, and highly-compensated-employee testing are ignored.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-normal text-stone-950 pt-4">Sources and methodology</h2>
  <p>
    Federal tax and FICA follow <code>calcPaycheck</code> and IRS Rev. Proc. 2025-32 data in <code>TAX_DATA_2026</code>. For elective deferral definitions and annual contribution limits, see IRS 401(k) plan guidance and Notice updates for the plan year. Educational estimate, not plan or tax advice.
  </p>
</GuideArticle>
